{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310612,"nonce":"X4P8x6QpK4gPGZvLnrwTMt3Ea8A=","title":"Replaced `mpsc::channel(...) + recv * n` with `AtomicUsize::fetch_sub + Mutex + Condvar` for the worker_pool::parallel_rows completion ...","message":"Per-call cost of the prior `parallel_rows`:\n- 2 allocs for `mpsc::channel + receiver`\n- 1 alloc for `Arc\u003cF\u003e`\n- 6 allocs for per-worker Box closures (one per worker)\n- Per-send + per-recv mpsc internal mutex ops\n\nHypothesis: replace channel with `AtomicUsize` countdown + a (Mutex,\nCondvar) pair for caller wakeup. Saves 2 allocs and replaces N send-\nmutex ops with N fetch_sub + 1 mutex+notify on the last worker.\n\nTheoretical win: ~5-10 µs per call × 7233 matmul calls = 36-72 ms = 1.6-3.3% of decode wall.\n\n\n|              | mean    | median |\n|--------------|---------|--------|\n| Pre-revert   | 34.10   | 34.85  |\n| Baseline     | 35.0    | 35.0   |\n| Δ            | -2.6%   | -0.4%  |\n\nThe atomic-countdown path is sub-noise neutral to slight regression vs\nthe existing mpsc path. matmul_qt_t per-call time was 265-302 µs/call\n(similar range to mpsc baseline ~270 µs/call).\n\n\n`std::mpsc::channel` is well-tuned in recent Rust — its internal\nsync uses lightweight primitives (crossbeam-derived) and the channel\nbuffer is allocated lazily / reused. The \"2 mallocs per call\" cost\nestimate was wrong: in steady state the channel impl avoids most\nallocations.\n\nAlso: contention on a single Mutex from all 6 workers at completion\ntime serialises them at the tail. The first try (Mutex\u003cusize\u003e\ndecrement under lock) produced 5-10 tok/s first-run regressions\nbecause of this. The fix (AtomicUsize::fetch_sub + only last worker\ntakes mutex) eliminated the contention but didn't beat mpsc.\n\n\nDon't optimise the fork-join sync primitive without instrumented\nevidence of its share of the call. `std::mpsc::channel` is good enough;\nreplacing it for \"fewer allocs in theory\" gives nothing measurable in\npractice on this workload.\n\nThe real fork-join overhead reduction would require either:\n- A typed job queue (skip the `Box\u003cdyn FnOnce\u003e` per worker) — bigger refactor\n- Scoped threads (skip the `Arc\u003cF\u003e` + `'static` requirement) — incompatible with the long-lived worker pool\n- Reduce call count via kernel fusion (already tried, mostly washed out)\n\n\nThis is the 5th \"mirror existing pattern\" / \"obvious infra improvement\"\nwash-out this session:\n\n1. [[bugs-q6k-partial-unroll-no-win]]\n2. [[bugs-f32-flash-gqa-no-win]]\n3. [[bugs-swiglu-fusion-no-win]]\n4. [[bugs-q4km-4block-paired-no-win]]\n5. This one\n\nCommon lesson: **measure the actual overhead BEFORE writing the\nreplacement**. Quick micro-instrumentation that times the specific\nprimitive (e.g. parallel_rows entry/exit minus the body) would have\ncaught this as sub-noise inside an hour. Wrote ~50 LOC + 7 runs to\nlearn what 1 timer probe would have told us.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_forkjoin_atomic_countdown_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}